CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with an acute, severe autonomic panic response, including marked tachycardia (HR 112), elevated blood pressure (140/88), and tachypnea (RR 24) triggered by a discriminatory public encounter. Immediate out-of-hours face-to-face assessment was critical to conduct a thorough cardiopulmonary examination, definitively rule out an acute myocardial infarction or other life-threatening cardiopulmonary event, and administer immediate pharmacological relief to break the hyperventilation cycle. Delaying assessment would have perpetuated severe physical distress and reinforced the trauma of medical avoidance, firmly justifying the urgent intervention.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered a single dose of oral lorazepam 1mg to immediately down-regulate the sympathetic nervous system and break the respiratory panic cycle. Advised the usual GP to check elevated allostatic load markers and review survey data reflecting high daily micro-stressor accumulation.
Positive Behavioral Change: To manage public affection hypervigilance, practice a 3-minute "co-regulation breathing" exercise with the partner before leaving the house: standing face-to-face, synchronize breathing to a 4-second inhale and 6-second exhale; this physical connection establishes a baseline of physiological safety before entering unpredictable environments. To combat coming-out fatigue in medical settings, utilize a pre-written "medical introduction card" detailing correct pronouns and family structure to hand to receptionists; this removes the burden of verbal explanation and preserves cognitive energy.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to an LGBTQIA+ affirmative counselor.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review within 48 hours for a resting ECG and comprehensive psychological care plan.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department or contact 000 if the chest pain becomes crushing and radiates to the jaw or arm, if experiencing severe dizziness leading to fainting, or if shortness of breath continues despite rest and medication.

